<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=trepanning">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trepanning</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6500 BCE</span> · Also known as Trepanation, Trephination</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trepanning, or trepanation, is a surgical intervention in which a hole is drilled or scraped into the human skull, perhaps the oldest surgical procedure with archaeological evidence; at a burial site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> dated to 6500 BCE, 40 of 120 skulls had trepanation holes. More than 1,500 trephined skulls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and many show that the patient survived. Performed with tools of stone, obsidian, bronze or bone, it was described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and survives in modern craniotomy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trepanning.jpg" alt="Trepanning" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trepanated_skull_of_a_woman-P4140363-black.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The word trepan comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>, from Medieval Latin trepanum and Greek trúpanon, meaning borer or auger. The operation exposes the dura mater and was mainly performed by priests or healers. Its purposes may have included treating intracranial disease, releasing blood that pooled after an injury, removing splinters of bone and relieving pressure. In some societies it was believed to release the evil spirits blamed for abnormal behaviour. Archaeologists identify a trephined skull by the hole, the shape of its edges and signs of healing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Early tools were hand-held and made of flint, obsidian or harder stone, and later of bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. In early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> the tumi, a ceremonial knife, was used, and in the South Pacific sharpened seashells. Five methods are recorded: rectangular intersecting cuts, scraping, circular grooving, cutting with a circular trephine or crown saw, and boring a ring of holes and chiselling between them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were the first to design instruments for the purpose, such as the terebra serrata.</p><p>Among the Andean peoples, including the Paracas culture, the Muisca and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, skulls with trepanation are common, between 11 and 40 per cent in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and many holes avoid the midline of the skull, which suggests a wish to reduce risk. Chinese examples include a skull from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> dated to about 5,000 years ago. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> populations had success rates of 78 per cent, and seven of eight skulls of the 6th to 8th centuries from south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> show healing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the 1870s the French anthropologist Paul Broca found several European and South American children's skulls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> that had been perforated surgically. He found no fractures to explain the operation and proposed a ritual or religious purpose, perhaps to remove confined demons or to make talismans from the removed bone. He also suggested it might treat childhood conditions such as febrile seizures. The idea that the holes treated headaches spread in the 19th century, but this belief is a myth.</p><p>In modern medicine trepanation is the ancestor of the craniotomy, used for epidural and subdural haematomas. Since the 1960s a few people have drilled holes in their own skulls, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> librarian Bart Huges in 1965, who claimed it increased brain blood volume. Research has not supported these claims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trepanning” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Paracas culture shows the earliest <b>trepanations</b> known in the Americas: parts of the skull were scraped and drilled away, and by the healing of the bone nearly 40 per cent of patients survived long-term.</blockquote><a href="/culture/paracas-culture">Paracas culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Paracas people practised cranial deformation and <b>trepanation</b> of the skull.</blockquote><a href="/state/paracas">Paracas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 6500 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Estimates of the earliest trepanation vary from 10,000 to 7,000 years ago.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=trepanning">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
